      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, we need to convert the angle from degrees to radians. We can do this using the formula: $\\text{radians} = \\text{degrees} \\times \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{180}\\right)$. Plugging in the given angle, we get $\\cos 135^\\circ = \\cos \\left(135^\\circ \\times \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{180}\\right)\\right) = \\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)$.\n\nNext, we need to find the cosine of $\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$. We can use the unit circle to find the coordinates of the point on the unit circle corresponding to this angle. The point is $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nNow, we can use the Pythagorean identity to find the cosine of the angle. The Pythagorean identity states that $\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1$. Plugging in the coordinates, we get $\\cos^2 \\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\sin^2 \\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1$. Solving for $\\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)$, we get $\\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nSince the angle is in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative, we have $\\cos \\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\cos 135^\\circ = \\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}$.",
